    <h1>About</h1>
    <p class="prose-lead">
      Over the past decade, I've spent more time with DNA sequences than with most people I know. And I wouldn't have it any other way.
    </p>

    <p>
      I'm <strong>Kalonji</strong> — a genomicist, computational biologist, and data scientist. I work at the intersection of <strong>genomics, health, and data</strong>, building computational tools that help us understand human genetic diversity and move closer to truly personalised medicine.
    </p>

    <p>
      Here's what drives me: <strong>we can't claim precision medicine is precise if it's built on data from a fraction of the world's populations.</strong> African genomes — the most genetically diverse on the planet — are still massively underrepresented in global databases. That's not just a gap in the science. It's a gap in who gets to benefit from it.
    </p>

    <p>
      My research combines <strong>population genomics, multi-omics integration, and data science</strong> to start closing that gap. I develop computational frameworks for population-scale datasets, characterise genetic variation in understudied communities, and apply machine learning to large biological datasets — all with the goal of making genomic insights more inclusive and more actionable.
    </p>

    <p>
      I earned my <strong>PhD in Molecular Biology</strong> from the University of the Witwatersrand, where I also completed my MSc (with Distinction), Honours, and postgraduate diploma. My training spans both the wet lab and the command line — from PCR and protein purification to building NGS analysis pipelines and mining LC-MS/MS proteomics data.
    </p>

    <p>
      Beyond the bench and the terminal, I've had the privilege of presenting at the <strong>Global Young Scientist Summit 2025</strong>, earning the <strong>Alex von Holy Prize</strong> (twice), and being recognised as a <strong>National Research Foundation</strong> doctoral scholar. I also teach — and my students have been kind enough to call me the "most perspicuous" and "most relatable" lecturer, which I take as the highest possible compliment.
    </p>
  </div>

